// std.core.scope
//
// `@scope` is the enclosing block as a value — what a LoopHandle is
// to a loop, one level down. Nests, each block gets its own:
//
//   {
//       outer = @scope;
//       {
//           inner = @scope;
//           inner.defer(() { println("inner cleanup") });
//       }                                    // inner's defers run
//       outer.defer(() { println("outer cleanup") });
//   }                                        // outer's defers run
//
// This is where `defer` lives — why it needs neither a keyword nor
// ambient authority: the block owns its own stack of closures. Env
// doesn't own one: it outlives every block inside main, storage in the
// wrong place. A Scope is NON-ESCAPING: passed INWARD so a helper can
// register cleanup on its caller's block, never stored, returned, or
// captured by an escaping closure — the same distinction that governs
// `.try()` and `h.break()`. Stage 3 is where the checker enforces it.
Scope* = {
    // required (`= sig`, no body): the compiler supplies the block.
    //
    // Registers a closure on THIS block; they run LIFO at block exit,
    // BEFORE any drop — a stack, not a map, since order is the point.
    //
    // `:` not `::`: the closure stack lives in the block, not the
    // handle's own bytes. A bitwise copy of the handle would still
    // see the registration — exactly the test.
    defer* = (self: @Self, f: () ()) ()
}
